Server-Sent Events (SSE): A one-way connection where the server continuously pushes updates to the client. Ideal for live dashboards, news feeds, notifications, and monitoring systems.

How it works:

|--------------------------------------------------|

Client                         Server
  │                              │
  │──── HTTP Request ───────────►│
  │      Open SSE Connection     │
  │                              │
  │◄──── Event / Update ─────────│
  │◄──── Event / Update ─────────│
  │◄──── Event / Update ─────────│
  │◄──── Event / Update ─────────│
  │                              │

|--------------------------------------------------|

he connection remains open, allowing the server to send new events whenever data changes.

Key Characteristics:
One-way communication: Server → Client
Uses a persistent HTTP connection
Supports real-time updates
Suitable for continuous event streams

Common Use Cases:
Real-time dashboards
Live news feeds
Notifications
Monitoring systems
Live status updates
Live scores
Streaming server-side events


SSE vs WebSocket:


SSE
Server ─────────────────► Client
       One-way


WebSocket
Server ◄────────────────► Client
       Two-way


For example, a live stock-price dashboard that only receives price updates can use SSE, 
while a trading application that needs continuous two-way communication may prefer WebSockets.
